# CPU Quotas and Execution Sandboxing via the MaxCycles Attribute

## Abstract
This paper presents the theoretical design of central processing unit (CPU) quotas in the openOODA architecture. The system uses a `MaxCycles` attribute to limit the execution time of functions. This attribute prevents untrusted code from freezing the compiler or the runtime environment. The design combines static analysis with dynamic runtime fuel metering. This dual approach ensures that programs stop safely. We outline the problem of unbounded loops. We also analyze related work and detail the hybrid enforcement architecture.

## 1. Introduction
The openOODA architecture needs strong defenses against infinite loops and excessive resource use. Developers can add a `#[MaxCycles(N)]` tag to functions. This tag sets a maximum limit on computational steps. A static analyzer tries to mathematically prove that the loop limits will finish under that maximum limit. If the analyzer cannot make this proof, the system uses runtime mechanisms.

Several failures can occur without CPU quotas. A simple infinite loop in a proposed patch can stop the developer workflow. A superlinear parser can cause a denial of service on hostile input. Accidental exponential recursion can exhaust the stack and time limits. Also, smart-contract guest code needs strict execution limits.

The core research question asks how to combine static termination proofs with runtime fuel metering. This combination must fit a systems language. It must also keep predictable performance. This paper separates the concept of static totality from practical fuel metering.

## 2. Related Work
The problem of worst-case execution time occurs in academia and industry. Static timing analysis for real-time systems often uses hardware models and loop annotations. Commercial tools and research projects show that full static proofs need language subsets. They also need strict hardware models. Thus, a general-purpose language needs a hybrid enforcement model.

Academic research on termination explores sized types and refinement types. Some languages enforce termination by design. Other systems can sometimes prove loop metrics. Resource-aware models can infer polynomial limits.

In blockchain and commercial systems, gas metering gives a dynamic fuel limit. The Ethereum Virtual Machine uses gas for each opcode. It also has a mechanism to revert when gas runs out. The Fuel Virtual Machine uses explicit fuel for parallel execution. WebAssembly systems use store-level fuel limits. Gas represents the most successful commercial version of the `MaxCycles` concept. It gives dynamic predictability for untrusted code. But it does not give a full worst-case execution time proof. Operating systems also give coarse process-level limits through timers.

## 3. Architecture and Methodology
The openOODA architecture uses a multi-tiered method for CPU quotas. The primary mechanism is the `MaxCycles` attribute. For example, a function that parses a header might have a limit of 5000 cycles.

The theoretical ideal is a static proof. The compiler analyzes every execution path. It ensures the path uses no more than the specified number of cycles. If the compiler cannot prove this limit, it must reject the code or use runtime enforcement.

The practical architecture uses a ladder of enforcement. The highest tier is a formal cost proof for an annotated subset of the language. The second tier uses static checks for obvious unbounded loops. The third tier uses runtime fuel. This fuel decreases during execution. It triggers a cycle error when the fuel reaches zero. The lowest tier relies on operating system processor limits as a backup.

The definition of a cycle relies on an abstract cost model. This model does not depend on specific hardware operations. Each intermediate representation operation costs one baseline cycle. Memory operations cause additional cycle costs. System calls have large fixed costs or separate metering mechanisms.

Native compiled code cannot naturally see this cost model. Thus, the compiler must insert fuel checks. Alternatively, the system can run untrusted code in a virtual machine. It can also use a WebAssembly environment that tracks fuel.

This architecture interacts closely with other resource limits. Wall-clock limits and cycle limits operate together. The system treats memory allocation limits as a dual resource limit. Fuzzing environments need a strict cycle budget for each input. Total functional correctness also helps to verify contract statements.

The error model dictates three outcomes. The static analyzer rejects code if it cannot prove the limit. The dynamic system recovers safely through sandboxed hosts when a cycle error occurs. Finally, the design dictates that claimed sandboxed entry points must never loop silently.

## 4. Threat and Failure Model
The `MaxCycles` architecture prevents several critical issues. It stops cooperative freezes in pure computation by enforcing fuel limits. It stops algorithmic denial of service attacks. It also stops runaway loops during testing or fuzzing.

However, the architecture has known limits. The static proof of termination is undecidable for all possible programs. Therefore, the hybrid approach is necessary. Native interface code without instrumentation can still spin without fuel limits. Hardware interrupts and graphics processor kernels need a completely different model. Priority inversion and lock spinning stay possible. These issues need separate concurrency controls.

Adversarial cost-model attacks remain a threat. If the cost model gives a low cost to an expensive operation, an attacker can use this difference. The attacker can cause a denial of service. The system must treat cost tables as security-critical data. The architecture requires version control and adversarial review of these cost tables.

## 5. Conclusion
The CPU quota design in openOODA makes a strong mechanism for execution sandboxing. The system combines the `MaxCycles` attribute with a hybrid static and dynamic enforcement model. This combination prevents infinite loops in untrusted code. This architecture solves the limits of pure static analysis. It also solves the overhead of pure dynamic metering. The resulting system can safely host multi-tenant evaluations. It can execute agent-generated code without risking host stability.

